## Wikipedia:Signs of AI writing

The structural and formatting pattern taxonomy in `skills/de-llm/SKILL.md` draws
on [Wikipedia:Signs of AI writing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Signs_of_AI_writing),
maintained by WikiProject AI Cleanup and licensed
[CC BY-SA 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/).

Several category names are Wikipedia's terminology and are used as such:
negative parallelism, superficial analysis with -ing endings, undue emphasis on
significance, copula avoidance, vague attribution, elegant variation, false
ranges, and the "challenges and future prospects" formula. The per-vendor
artifact strings in Tier 4 are also compiled there.

The text here is a paraphrase and restructuring rather than a copy, and the
ordering, thresholds, rewrite guidance, and measurements are original. If you
reuse the pattern taxonomy itself, attribute Wikipedia.

## Kobak et al. 2025

Frequency ratios, absolute gaps, and the excess-vocabulary word lists in Tier 3
are reported from:

> Kobak, D., González-Márquez, R., Horvát, E.-Á., et al. (2025).
> "Delving into LLM-assisted writing in biomedical publications through excess
> vocabulary." *Science Advances*. Preprint: [arXiv:2406.07016](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.07016).
> Data and code: <https://github.com/berenslab/llm-excess-vocab>

These are cited findings, not a derivative work. See
`skills/de-llm/references/sources.md` for what the paper does and does not
establish.

## Own measurements

The corpus measurements in `references/sources.md` (35 plugin READMEs, 2,298
plugin descriptions) are original to this project and MIT licensed with the
rest. They have no baseline and measure prevalence rather than excess. Read the
limitations before citing them.
